course: history
lesson: 69
title: Rights won step by step: freedom, votes and children's rights
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Ethan's friend says slavery ended in 1865, done. Jaden thinks Rosa Parks just sat down on a bus and changed everything alone. Ella asks: once rights are won, they stay, right? All three stories are too neat.
explain: What really happened: Britain banned slavery in most of its empire in 1834, the US in 1865, Brazil in 1888 and Mauritania only in 1981, and forced labour still happens illegally today. Rosa Parks was part of a planned movement with many people. And rights can be lost: Germany's democracy was ended in 1933 when Hitler's party took power.
explain: Think of a staircase: each step was won by people who pushed for it. Serfs, farmers tied to a landlord's land, were freed in the Russian Empire, including much of Ukraine, only in 1861. Kiri from New Zealand says her country let women vote first, in 1893, «sweet as», Kiwi for great.
explain: Children's rights are a new step. In the 1800s children worked in mines and mills; in 1989 the UN agreed the Convention on the Rights of the Child. Disabled people campaigned for ramps and access, and rulers like the Mughal emperor Akbar showed long ago that respecting other faiths helps peace.
explain: Some steps took decades. Nelson Mandela spent 27 years in prison under apartheid, South Africa's laws separating people by race, then became president in 1994. Rights are like a garden: they need looking after, or they grow over.
fact: Serfs were farmers tied to a landlord's land who could not leave freely; serfdom ended in the Austrian Empire in 1848 and in the Russian Empire, including much of Ukraine, in 1861.
fact: Britain banned the slave trade in 1807 and slavery in most of its empire in 1834; the US ended slavery in 1865, Brazil in 1888, and Mauritania made it illegal in 1981; forced labour still happens illegally today.
fact: New Zealand gave women the vote in 1893; women in Switzerland got the national vote only in 1971, and many countries lowered the voting age from 21 to 18 around 1970.
fact: In 1989 the UN agreed the Convention on the Rights of the Child, promising school, safety, health and a voice; almost every country in the world has joined it.
fact: In the 1800s many children worked in factories and mines; Britain's Factory Act of 1833 banned children under 9 from textile mills, and well over 100 million children still work today, mostly on farms.
fact: Eleanor Roosevelt chaired the group that wrote the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted in 1948, with people from many countries such as René Cassin of France and Peng Chun Chang of China.
fact: In 1955 Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat in Alabama; the bus boycott that followed, led by Martin Luther King and others, helped end segregation laws, which kept Black and white people apart on buses, in schools and in shops.
fact: Nelson Mandela was released in 1990 after 27 years in prison and elected president in 1994 in South Africa's first election open to all races.
fact: Disabled people campaigned for access; the US passed the Americans with Disabilities Act in 1990, and the UN agreed a convention on disabled people's rights in 2006.
fact: Akbar, who ruled the Mughal Empire in India in the late 1500s, held debates with Hindus, Muslims, Christians, Jains and others, and ended a special tax on non-Muslims.
fact: Rights can be lost: Germany was a democracy after 1919, but Hitler's party took power in 1933 and ended it.
exercise: What was a serf? | A farmer tied to a landlord's land | A king's soldier who guarded the castle | A sailor on a big trade ship | A teacher in a monastery
exercise: When did serfdom end in the Russian Empire, including much of Ukraine? | In 1861 | In 1066 | In 1991 | In 1215
exercise: When did the US end slavery? | In 1865 | In 1776 | In 1981 | In 1066
exercise: Which country made slavery illegal only in 1981? | Mauritania | Britain, after a long debate | Brazil, the last in the Americas | New Zealand
exercise: Which country first let women vote, in 1893? | New Zealand | Switzerland, in the Alps | France, after the revolution | Ancient Egypt
exercise: When did women in Switzerland get the national vote? | In 1971 | In 1893 | In 1215 | In 1800
exercise: What did the UN agree in 1989? | The Convention on the Rights of the Child | The first Geneva Convention on wounded soldiers | Magna Carta, the charter of English barons | The Factory Act for children in British mills
exercise: Where did many children work in the 1800s? | In factories and mines | In offices with computers | In space stations | Nowhere, they all went to school
exercise: What did Britain's Factory Act of 1833 do? | Banned children under 9 from textile mills | Closed every school in Britain for good | Made children work longer days in mills | Gave children over nine the vote
exercise: Who chaired the group that wrote the Universal Declaration of Human Rights? | Eleanor Roosevelt | Rosa Parks of Alabama | Nelson Mandela | Queen Victoria of Britain
exercise: What did Rosa Parks refuse to do in 1955? | Give up her bus seat | Pay her taxes to the state | Go to her school | Vote in an election for mayor
exercise: What helped end segregation laws after Rosa Parks's protest? | A bus boycott by many people | One speech by the president on television | A war between America and Britain | A football match between two towns
exercise: How long was Nelson Mandela in prison? | 27 years | Only 27 days | About 2 years | Over 72 years
exercise: When was Mandela elected president? | In 1994 | In about 1964 | In 1948, very young | In 2010
exercise: What did the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990 help bring? | Access to buildings, schools and work | Free cars for every family in America | A ban on ramps in all new buildings | The end of all other laws on work
exercise: What did the Mughal emperor Akbar do? | Held debates between many faiths | Banned every religion in India | Built the first buses in old Delhi | Started the Red Cross in India
exercise: What happened to Germany's democracy in 1933? | Hitler's party took power and ended it | It became the very first democracy in the world | It moved its parliament to Iceland | Nothing, it stayed exactly the same
test: Danylo's grandma says their great-great-grandparents were serfs. What would they not be allowed to do? | Leave the landlord's land freely | Eat bread or grow any vegetables | Sing songs in church on Sundays | Have names of their own
test: «slavery ended 1865 done» says Ethan's mate. What would Ethan say? | Not everywhere; it ended at different times and forced labour still exists | Yeah, correct: it ended everywhere in the world on the very same day | Nah, it actually ended back in 1066, with the Normans | Nah, slavery never really existed in the first place
test: Kiri says NZ was first for women's votes, sweet as. Why did Swiss women wait until 1971? | Rights came step by step, at different times in different countries | Swiss women simply did not want to vote until 1971 | The Swiss had no elections at all before 1971 | They didn't wait: Swiss women voted first of all, in 1800, before anyone
test: Lily asks since when kids have rights. What would happen without the 1989 Convention? | Children would have weaker promises of school, safety and a voice | Children would get the vote | Nothing, kids always had all rights | Adults would lose their rights
test: Ryan: «kids my age worked in MINES?? no way». Is child labour over? | No; well over 100 million children still work, mostly on farms | Yes, it ended everywhere in the world in 1833 | It never happened, it's just in old stories | Yes, robots do all the work now, so kids don't have to anymore
test: Grace asks what Eleanor Roosevelt did. Were human rights written by one country? | No, people from many countries wrote them together | Yes, only by America, and the rest just signed it | Yes, only by France, after its revolution | They were never written down, only spoken
test: Jaden says Rosa Parks just sat down and changed everything by herself. Fair? | She was brave, and part of a planned movement with many people | Yes, nobody else helped her at all, she did it all on her own | She was really the bus driver, not a passenger | It happened in 1066, so nobody remembers
test: Thabo asks how Mandela went from prison to president. What is the truest summary? | Decades of struggle ended apartheid, then all races voted in 1994 | He escaped from prison and took over the government by force | He was president first, then he went to prison for 27 years | It all happened in one week in 1994
test: Alfie asks why some buildings have no ramps. What would a campaigner say? | Access was won by campaigning, and some old places still lack it | Ramps are not allowed by law in most old buildings in Britain | Wheelchairs were only invented last year | Nobody needs ramps, so builders leave them out
test: Aarav says Akbar let all faiths talk, like a group chat. Why was this wise for his empire? | Respecting many faiths helped a mixed empire stay peaceful | It made everyone in the empire follow the very same religion | It stopped all trade, which kept the empire quiet | It made him lose his throne at once to his son
test: Ella thinks rights won always stay. If people stop protecting rights, what can happen? | They can be lost, as in Germany in 1933 | Nothing, rights last for ever | Rights get stronger alone | Rights turn into money
test: In EU4 you click a button to «abolish serfdom». In real life, what did it take? | Decades of pressure, reform and struggle by many people | One click by one king, and it was done that same afternoon | Nothing, serfs just freed themselves overnight | A football match between the serfs and the lords
exam: Identify the year in which serfdom was abolished in the Russian Empire, inclusive of much of present-day Ukraine. | Eighteen sixty-one | Eighteen forty-eight | Nineteen eighty-one | Seventeen seventy-six
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the abolition of slavery. | It was abolished at different dates in different countries, and forced labour persists illegally | It was abolished everywhere simultaneously in 1865, by agreement among all governments of the world | It was never practised outside Europe at any time | It was abolished in Mauritania in 1807, before all other countries
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the drafting of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights? | It was prepared by persons from many countries under the chairmanship of Eleanor Roosevelt | It was written by a single French lawyer and adopted by every government without amendment | It was proclaimed by a medieval monarch in the year 1215 | It was drafted exclusively by Chinese scholars
exam: Ascertain the lesson most accurately drawn from the ending of German democracy in 1933. | Rights and democratic institutions may be lost unless protected | Democracies cannot ever end | Rights are guaranteed in perpetuity | Elections are invariably unnecessary
